WEBVTT

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:03.910
We know that Akutagawa Ryuunosuke,

00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:06.100
who looked up to Natsume 
Soseki as his mentor,

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.600
is considered the greatest short story 
author in modern Japanese literature...

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.280
Sequential Control
Circuits
Practical
Applications

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.530
<i>Just who was that girl, anyway?</i>

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:22.200
<i>I took the book she left behind because it 
looked like it was hers, not a library book,</i>

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.920
<b>Quiz Bowl Buzzer Lamp Circuits</b>

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.310
<i>but these marked pages 
have piqued my curiosity.</i>

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:27.250
<i>Is she involved in quiz bowl, too?</i>

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:29.660
Koshiyama.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.310
Koshiyama!

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:31.880
Uh, yes, sir!

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:33.710
Are you listening?

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:35.280
U-Uh, yes, sir.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.900
Then, briefly explain the signature qualities 
of Akutagawa Ryuunosuke's works.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.420
R-Right...

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:46.430
<i>"Briefly explain"? How vague 
an instruction can you get?</i>

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:50.610
<i>Is he talking about how most of 
Akutagawa's works are based on classics?</i>

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:55.570
<i>No, they had deep roots in Western 
literature of the time, too...</i>

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.160
What's the problem, Koshiyama?

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:03.720
<i>I'm just not good at organizing my 
thoughts and putting them into words.</i>

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.080
<i>If you'd asked a more specific question, 
I could have answered it easily...</i>

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.530
See? This is what happens when 
you don't listen in class!

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.210
No, I do know!

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:14.970
<i>Yeah, for example...</i>

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.800
If you phrased it more 
like a quiz bowl question...

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.760
That idiot.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:22.720
Do you think this is a joke?!

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.320
I-I'm sorry!

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:59.450
<b>Question 5: "Buzou vs Miyaura! Ding!"</b>

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:57.030
Gone!

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:57.610
Gone!

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:58.490
Gone!

00:02:58.530 --> 00:02:59.450
Gone...

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.750
My prized book...

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:04.680
Did that kid who shared his 
lunch with me pick it up?

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.160
Wonder if he's still at school...

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:09.160
Marina, where are we going again?

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.560
The roof for voice projection practice.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:14.130
What was that you said about quiz bowl?

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.150
You're hilarious, man.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.880
Er... I, uh...

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.030
Are you so buddy-buddy with Fukami Mari 
that she influences you that much?

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:24.390
It's no laughing matter.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.990
Why did I say that?

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:29.140
Seriously, you're so embarrassing!

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:33.420
You're just making trouble for quiz bowl
circle members like Fukami-san and me!

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.270
What, you're in the quiz bowl circle, too?

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:38.740
That's right! Just like Fukami-san!

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:41.390
And I'm the class librarian, 
just like Fukami-san, too!

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:44.900
So he's not the one who's 
buddy-buddy with Fukami-san—

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:45.370
Anyway, I should get to practice.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:46.660
He wasn't even listening...

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.500
Hey, you're both still in here!

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.520
Let's get to quiz bowl!

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:53.500
Today, we have...

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.130
...a joint practice with another 
school's quiz team!

00:03:57.710 --> 00:03:58.880
Joint practice?

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.010
Which school?

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.250
I haven't heard which one it is,

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:03.510
but isn't it exciting?

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.400
Maybe it's Asagaoka Girls' High School?

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:09.130
Um, you two go on ahead.

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:10.890
I'm gonna stop by the faculty office.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:14.480
Roger! We'll be waiting
 for you, Koshiyama-kun!

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:19.510
<i>Fukami-san really perks up when 
there's any mention of quiz bowl.</i>

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.860
<i>Though, I guess I can't talk...</i>

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:28.240
<i>Am I going to think of everything 
in terms of quiz bowl soon, too?</i>

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:30.870
<i>Quiz bowl is scary...</i>

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:35.240
<i>Joint practice, huh? I wonder 
which school they're coming from.</i>

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:37.840
"Small Classroom B"?

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:39.710
Where's B?

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:40.930
Dunno.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:43.220
It's the room your quiz team uses.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.600
"Quiz team"? Does he mean 
the quiz bowl circle?

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.390
That's actually active?

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.510
Uh, never mind. Thanks.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.230
I guess quiz teams aren't that 
well-known at any school.

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:57.370
<i>Ours is a little more 
recognized than that, though.</i>

00:04:57.370 --> 00:04:59.950
<i>I should've told them to wait for me 
when I went to the bathroom.</i>

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.740
Pardon me.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:05.030
There he is!

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:05.030
R-Ragnarok!

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.280
M-Mikuriya-kun! Why are you here?

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.300
Oh... The joint practice?

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.130
Yes! Hurry up and show me 
where "Small Classroom B" is!

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:14.540
S-Sure...

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:18.070
What's going on with your 
school's quiz team, anyway?

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:19.840
No one even knows about it.

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.570
<i>So the school was Miyaura?</i>

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:26.910
<i>Well, I guess they're only 
in the next town over.</i>

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:31.180
<i>Does this mean I'll get to see 
Mikuriya-kun's super fast buzzing again?</i>

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:32.930
<i>I can't wait!</i>

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:35.290
<i>Wait, we use a calculator! 
What're we gonna do?!</i>

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:36.960
<i>What's with this guy?</i>

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:39.060
<b>Small Classroom B</b>

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.570
Yo. Sorry I'm late.

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:44.630
Hey, Mikuriya. That was
 a long bathroom break.

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:46.030
I got lost!

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.740
It appears everyone's here now.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:52.120
As requested, these are our new members.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:53.490
Maruyama.

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:54.700
Mukai.

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:55.910
Kiyosumi.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:56.890
Mikuriya.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:57.870
Also...

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.330
Wow!

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:00.850
Look! Real buzzers!

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:02.160
It's not a calculator!

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:03.460
It's so cool to see real buzzers!

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:04.920
Uh, <i>that's</i> what grabs you?

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:09.460
I will now announce today's quiz rules.

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:13.360
No one's listening...

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.450
Well, buzzer systems are of primary 
concern to every new quiz team.

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:19.130
They're not sold just anywhere,

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:23.720
and not every school has a set that's 
been passed down through years like ours.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:24.860
That's true.

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:27.070
All I have is...

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.030
this single button.

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:32.950
Huh? Why do you have just that one?

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:34.370
Wow, a white button...

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:37.660
Someone gave me this a long time ago...

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:40.580
But it's missing its cord. It's broken.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.040
I only keep it, out of sight like this,

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:45.300
<b>Click
 Click</b>

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:47.050
to keep myself familiar with the 
feel of the button on a daily basis.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.050
<b>Click 
Click</b>

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:47.990
<i>Yikes...</i>

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:48.990
<i>Scary...</i>

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:50.480
That's so cool!

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:52.300
<i>That's cool?!</i>

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:55.070
Anyway, aren't we gonna some quizzing?

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:56.460
It'll be dark soon.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:00.020
All right, let's move the desks.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:06.250
Today, we'll have a team match 
between the two schools.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:08.570
Though, we could have played free, as well...

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:10.150
"Free"?

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:11.430
"Free batting."

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:13.600
It's a buzz-in match

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.950
where you don't set any rules, 
or if you do, they're simple ones.

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:19.510
Since we're all here for 
a joint practice session,

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:22.700
why not have a team match pitting the 
schools against each other for the fun of it?

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.360
Just like those TV quiz bowl shows.

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.020
A team match, huh?

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:29.730
Wait, what about Mikuriya-kun?

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:32.750
I'm not playing.

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:35.930
The gulf in skill is too big, I guess? 
Wouldn't want to mess up your game.

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:38.470
He's so full of himself.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:39.890
Why did he even tag along?

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:41.980
Oh... That's too bad.

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:44.610
Are you nuts? He'd pulverize us!

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:47.820
<i>This isn't the place for 
my match against you.</i>

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.030
As I was saying, I will 
now explain the rules.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:55.340
This will be a "fastest finger" contest. 
You'll have five seconds to deliberate.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.100
For every correct answer, 
you earn one point.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:04.360
For every incorrect answer, 
you lose one point.

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:07.540
<b>Miyaura</b>

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:07.540
<b>Buzou</b>

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:07.540
The first team to ten points wins.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:10.670
Those rules are meant to 
test our team's cohesion.

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:12.690
You'll each have a buzzer,

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:15.540
but the whole team will 
have the right to answer.

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:19.060
In other words, after buzzing, 
anyone on your team can answer.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:20.060
Can we confer?

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:22.590
No. No conferring.

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:24.270
Also...

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:27.250
If two players give the same 
answer simultaneously,

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:30.410
it will automatically be treated as 
an incorrect answer, so be careful.

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:35.910
In general, it's best if the person who 
buzzes in is also responsible for answering.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:39.870
Ashiya-san and I came up with the questions, 
based on those used in past quizzes.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:41.910
Hey, this might be our chance!

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:44.580
<i>I just need to buzz if I know it, right?</i>

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:48.240
<i>But if I guess wrong, 
the team loses a point...</i>

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:50.460
<i>I need to be careful.</i>

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:53.200
We'll start with the buzzer test.

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:58.670
<i>Sasajima-senpai said not to 
focus on winning and losing,</i>

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:02.310
<i>but in a match like this, the team that 
doesn't focus on being victorious can't win.</i>

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:05.980
<i>That's exactly it! Quiz bowl is...</i>

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:10.980
Miyaura 
vs 
Buzou

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:08.020
<i>a competitive world!</i>

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:12.990
It's not a 
calculator!

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:12.990
It's not a 
calculator!

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:12.990
It's not a 
calculator!

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:12.990
A calculator?

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:16.840
Question.

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:19.740
Lincoln said, "Government of 
the people, by the people—

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:21.200
Gettysburg!

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:22.830
<b>Q: Lincoln said, "Government of the people, by the people, for the 
people" in a famous speech delivered in which Pennsylvanian city? 
A: Gettysburg</b>

00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:22.780
Correct.

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:25.210
<i>The name of the city where 
the speech was given?</i>

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:26.460
<b>Buzou</b>

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:26.460
<b>Miyaura</b>

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:27.770
Question.

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:30.140
Italian for "small goose"—

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:30.880
Ocarina!

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:32.210
Correct.

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:33.130
<b>Q: Italian for "small goose," the instrument invented in the 
19th century by Giuseppe Donati is known by what name?  
A: Ocarina</b>

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:34.260
Yes!

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:35.050
Awesome!

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:37.590
<i>I think Fukami-san may win 
this for us all by herself...</i>

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:40.800
<b>Buzou</b>

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:40.800
<b>Miyaura</b>

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:40.800
<b>What's wrong 
with you guys?</b>

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:40.800
What's wrong with you guys?

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:43.270
<i>Buzou isn't gonna...</i>

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:45.230
<i>...beat us!</i>

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:45.230
<b>College Prep 
School Pride</b>

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:45.230
<b>No. 1 in the 
Prefecture</b>

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:46.470
Question.

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:50.650
The method of frying eggs in which only 
one side is fried is known as "sunny—

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:52.450
Turned over!

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:53.440
Correct.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:53.440
<b>Q: The method of frying eggs in which only one side is cooked 
is known as "sunny side up," and the method in which both sides 
are cooked is called what?                    A: Turned over</b>

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:55.530
Question.

00:09:55.990 --> 00:10:00.650
Fingerprints, voice prints, and other unique 
physical characteristics are used to—

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:02.580
Biometrics!

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:03.390
Correct.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:03.790
<b>Q: Fingerprints, voice prints, and other unique physical 
characteristics are used to verify individual identity in what 
authentication technique?                        A: Biometrics</b>

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:04.540
Question.

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:08.560
What technique, based on an Indonesian 
folk dance, is seen at idol—

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:09.850
Kecak!

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:11.030
Correct.

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:11.790
<b>Q: What technique, based on an Indonesian folk dance, is seen 
at idol concerts in the form of "otagei" performances?   A: Kecak</b>

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:13.800
That's supposed to be my specialty!

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.090
<b>Buzou</b>

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.090
<b>Miyaura</b>

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:19.010
<i>It's the same pattern again.</i>

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:20.560
<i>I can't even buzz in.</i>

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:23.570
<i>Does this "pushing in" thing really work?</i>

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:25.170
Question.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:28.890
In the Gospel According to 
Matthew in the New Testament,

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:31.160
which recounts Jesus Christ's 
"Sermon on the Mount,"

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:35.690
mankind is likened to 
"the <i>what</i> of the earth"?

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:40.310
<i>It's not a phrase people use often, but...</i>

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:44.330
The salt of the earth.

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:45.220
Correct.

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:46.750
Whoa, nice!

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:49.750
<b>In the Gospel According to Matthew in the New Testament, 
which recounts Jesus Christ's "Sermon on the Mount,
"mankind is likened to "the <i>what</i> of the earth"?      A: The salt of the earth</b>

00:10:47.270 --> 00:10:49.900
<i>He got a question that was 
about to go unanswered, huh?</i>

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:52.100
<i>I guess it's not bad having 
someone like that on a team.</i>

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:54.260
<i>Not good enough.</i>

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.590
<i>If you can't buzz in faster 
on the easy questions,</i>

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:58.360
<i>you won't make it past the early rounds.</i>

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:01.430
<i>And you'll never make it 
into the same ring as me.</i>

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:02.780
Question.

00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:06.820
The 17th Century Dutch artist known 
for <i>Girl with a Pearl Earring</i>—

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:09.230
Vermeer.

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:10.220
Correct.

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:10.940
<b>Q: The 17th Century Dutch artist known for <i>Girl with a Pearl 
Earring</i> and <i>The Milkmaid</i> was whom?               A: Vermeer</b>

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:15.110
<i>We'll get nowhere if we can't buzz in first.</i>

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:18.380
<i>He said these were Miyaura's new members,</i>

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:20.500
<i>but I guess they have 
the edge in cohesiveness?</i>

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:24.070
<i>It's up to me to try my hardest now.</i>

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:25.150
Question.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:27.200
<b>Buzou</b>

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:27.200
<b>Miyaura</b>

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:27.560
The faces of a die add up to t—

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:28.950
<i>"T"?</i>

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:30.410
<i>A parallel?!</i>

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:32.200
Five seconds.

00:11:33.330 --> 00:11:35.340
<i>They add up to twenty-one.</i>

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:37.950
<i>If Fukami-san is hesitating, then...</i>

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:39.110
<i>They add up to "t"...</i>

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:42.930
<i>The "twenty-one" is already in the question?</i>

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:46.950
<i>Which means subtracting or dividing would 
be unnatural, so maybe multiplying?</i>

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:50.300
<i>You've gotta memorize
 questions about dice.</i>

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:52.520
<i>It takes too long to do the math.</i>

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:54.540
<i>Six... twenty-four...</i>

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:56.480
<i>I can't work it out in time!</i>

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:57.730
720!

00:11:58.280 --> 00:11:59.190
Correct.

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:01.950
<b>Q: All the faces of a die add up to twenty-one, and multiplying 
them results in what number?               A: 720</b>

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:01.950
Leave the math questions to me!

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:03.950
Inoue-kun!

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:05.650
Yay!

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:06.870
That was great!

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:10.580
Die in a fire, normie!

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:14.040
<i>Yes. For the time being, this is fine.</i>

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:17.260
<i>Enjoy quiz bowl to your hearts' content.</i>

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:20.580
<i>One day, you'll take part 
in a real competition.</i>

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:21.800
<i>When that time comes...</i>

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:24.950
<i>what you feel now will be your sustenance.</i>

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:27.260
Let's keep it up!

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:28.000
Yeah!

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:32.400
Question.

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:36.350
Wind blowing from the land toward the sea 
is said to be favorable for ships—

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:37.310
<i>They won't take this one!</i>

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:38.670
Tailwind!

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:39.460
That one?

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:40.530
<i>Not forewind?</i>

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:42.990
<b>Q: Wind blowing from the land toward the sea is said to be favorable 
for ships leaving port, earning it what name?  X Tailwind  A: Forewind</b>

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:42.990
The correct answer is forewind.

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:43.890
Huh?!

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:44.840
<i>I thought so...</i>

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:46.370
Don't get all trigger-happy!

00:12:46.370 --> 00:12:48.580
<b>Miyaura</b>

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:48.580
Did you forget that 
wrong answers lose points?!

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:49.790
Question.

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:52.510
Which Japanese term, meaning 
"bustling activity," originated—

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:54.330
"Tenteko"—

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:55.670
"Touhon seisou!"

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:59.170
<b>Q: Which Japanese term, meaning "bustling activity," originated 
from Japanese drum performances?  X Touhon seisou  A: Tentekomai</b>

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:58.240
The correct answer is "tentekomai."

00:12:58.510 --> 00:12:59.430
Are you serious?!

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:03.510
<i>So "originated" didn't refer to a </i>place<i> of origin, 
but a </i>point<i> of origin in Japanese music.</i>

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:01.960
<b>Originated (place)</b>

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:03.920
<b>Originated (point)</b>

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:06.300
Mukai, you idiot! I buzzed in!

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:08.390
Well, you keep getting them wrong!

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:09.930
No conferring.

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:13.900
<i>Now's our chance!</i>

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:15.750
Question.

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:18.730
The Four Great Classical Novels 
of Chinese literature...

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:20.230
<i>Numerated format and literature!</i>

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:21.440
...are <i>Romance of the Three Kingdoms</i>...

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:23.420
<i>I don't know all three of the classics.</i>

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:25.660
<i>What I do know is the determining point:</i>

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:27.450
<i>The first syllable of the second title!</i>

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:28.160
...<i>Wa—</i>

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:31.570
<i>The determining point 
is on the third title.</i>

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:32.450
<i>That was a mistake.</i>

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:36.430
<i>It's not the "Three Great Classical 
Novels"! There are four of them!</i>

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:39.110
<i>I was going to pass this 
one off to Koshiyama-kun,</i>

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:41.410
<i>but I got the determining point wrong!</i>

00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:45.590
<i>Of course! This question's for me!</i>

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:49.520
<i>The hints given were </i>Romance of the 
Three Kingdoms<i> and </i>Water Margin.<i></i>

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:48.130
<b>Romance of the Three Kingdoms</b>

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:50.100
<b>Water Margin</b>

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:52.490
<i>That leaves </i>Journey to the West<i> 
and </i>The Plum in the Golden Vase.<i></i>

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:51.890
<b>Journey to the West</b>

00:13:52.220 --> 00:13:53.600
<b>The Plum in the Golden Vase</b>

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:55.420
<i>So which one's the right answer?</i>

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:59.490
Three, two, one...

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:00.530
<i>The Plum in the Golden Vase</i>!

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:04.230
<b>Q: The Four Great Classical Novels of Chinese literature are 
<i>Romance of the Three Kingdoms</i>, <i>Water Margin,</i> <i>Journey to the 
West</i>, and what?                  A: <i>The Plum in the Golden Vase</i></b>

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:02.760
Correct.

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:06.820
How did you decide which one it was?

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:09.410
Most people think of them as the 
"Three Great Classical Novels,"

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:13.030
which are <i>Romance of the Three Kingdoms</i>, 
<i>Water Margin,</i> and <i>Journey to the West</i>.

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:16.540
But this question specified four novels, 
so I figured it had to be that one.

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:20.960
<i>They saved me on both the 
numerated and parallel formats!</i>

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:23.970
Pushing in is making my hand cramp up.

00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:25.680
You've been doing that?

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:29.070
<i>And they both only just got into quiz bowl.</i>

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:31.660
<i>They're both amazing!</i>

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:33.970
<i>This is a surprise.</i>

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:38.870
Question.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:41.710
French for "new wave"—

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:44.190
<i>All three of them at once?!</i>

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:46.400
<i>Language questions are Fukami-san's thing!</i>

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:47.780
La Nouvelle Vague!

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:49.990
Correct.

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:50.950
<b>Q: French for "new wave," what term refers to a 1950s trend 
in French film-making?                 A: La Nouvelle Vague</b>

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:50.950
Yes!

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:52.410
<b>Buzou</b>

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:52.410
<b>Miyaura</b>

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:54.130
<i>Just three more points...</i>

00:14:56.550 --> 00:15:00.320
Dammit! We're supposed to 
be better on the buzzer!

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:02.280
It's because you keep
 getting trigger-happy!

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:04.540
If you hadn't shouted over
 me for that answer...

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:05.380
What?!

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:07.480
Hey, cool it.

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:08.880
If we get too flustered, we'll lose.

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:13.890
So far, all the questions have been 
ones we've seen in collections, right?

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:15.480
Y-Yeah.

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:16.680
That's true.

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:23.660
<i>We have mountains of past question 
collections left by older students and alumni.</i>

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:28.360
<i>I'm buried in them every day, 
but I've still only read a fraction.</i>

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:31.360
<i>We should have the advantage 
on questions used in past quizzes!</i>

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:32.900
Question.

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:35.600
"Float like a butterfly, sting like a—

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:37.670
Muhammad Ali.

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:38.550
Correct.

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:39.830
<b>Q: "Float like a butterfly, sting like a bee" was said to describe the 
style of which former heavyweight world champion boxer?
A: Muhammad Ali</b>

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:41.740
All right!

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:42.470
Nice!

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:43.330
Thanks.

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:45.290
<b>Buzou</b>

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:45.290
<b>Miyaura</b>

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:49.260
<i>We can't lose to a rookie quiz team 
that doesn't even have buzzers!</i>

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:51.680
<i>Miyaura's attitude has changed...</i>

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:56.710
It's okay.

00:15:57.700 --> 00:15:59.350
Just three more points.

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:00.310
Let's get them together!

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:03.360
Yeah!

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:07.480
Time to catch up!

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:08.230
Yeah!

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:10.740
Question.

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:13.110
Locally known as "Uluru"—

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:15.620
Ayers Rock!

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:17.050
Correct.

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:18.410
<i>In an instant,</i>

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:21.460
<i>it felt like everyone was 
suddenly on the same page.</i>

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:25.290
<i>Everyone participating becomes one</i>

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:26.770
<i>in pursuit of the questions...</i>

00:16:27.590 --> 00:16:29.050
<b>Miyaura</b>

00:16:27.590 --> 00:16:31.010
<i>That's all there is in this 
world we build together.</i>

00:16:37.030 --> 00:16:38.480
<i>Just one more point.</i>

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:40.140
Question.

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:47.090
Meaning "there is no longer friend or f—

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:51.110
No side!

00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:52.600
Correct.

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:56.570
<b>Buzou</b>

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:56.570
<b>Miyaura</b>

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:58.030
All right!

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:58.030
<b>Q: Meaning "there is no longer friend or foe," what is called to
 signal the end of a rugby match?           A: No side</b>

00:17:01.310 --> 00:17:04.510
Miyaura High School wins, 10-9.

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:09.630
We lost.

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:10.750
Still...

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:14.780
Thanks to you buzzing in, 
I was able to answer.

00:17:14.780 --> 00:17:15.590
Me, too!

00:17:19.330 --> 00:17:22.280
I answered a math question! 
Was that awesome or what?

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:24.430
Yeah. I wonder if that was a classic.

00:17:24.430 --> 00:17:25.060
I'll remember it.

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:28.580
<i>There's no need for quiz bowl 
that you play only to win.</i>

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:32.860
<i>When I was reminded of 
my own ability and panicking...</i>

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:36.590
<i>I nearly forgot what's really important.</i>

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:39.990
Fukami-san?

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:42.450
That was a fun quiz, wasn't it?

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:47.400
We won, and yet...

00:17:47.770 --> 00:17:49.630
Why does it feel like we lost?

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:54.310
Want to go one more round?

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:54.970
Sounds good.

00:17:56.330 --> 00:17:57.430
Move!

00:17:57.430 --> 00:17:59.950
You call yourselves members 
of the Miyaura quiz team?!

00:17:59.950 --> 00:18:03.180
And who even cares if you guys win or lose?!

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:05.570
M-Mikuriya's snapped!

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:07.190
And I don't even get why!

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:08.900
Let me play, too!

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:12.760
Hey, Buzou! You're up against me next!

00:18:12.760 --> 00:18:14.360
I'll take all three of you on by myself!

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:15.570
Sorry.

00:18:15.860 --> 00:18:18.840
Of course, I won't even let 
any of you push the—

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:24.200
Oh, s'pretty simplistic inside.

00:18:24.790 --> 00:18:25.450
Ain't it?

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:29.250
What have you done?!

00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:31.250
<i>The cute girl from before!</i>

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:34.710
Oh, and this one's got a lot packed into it!

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:44.350
Honestly... You can't just take 
apart other people's things!

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:46.630
I only opened 'em up a little.

00:18:46.630 --> 00:18:48.310
See? Nothin' wrong with 'em, is there?

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:52.440
Besides, you're the one who said 
you'd show me a real set, Gakuto!

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:54.650
<i>She called him by his first name?!</i>

00:18:55.780 --> 00:18:58.780
U-Um... What's your relationship 
to Sasajima-senpai?

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:00.990
Huh? You don't know?

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:03.280
Gakuto's my...

00:19:03.850 --> 00:19:05.540
Your...

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:07.250
...big brother!

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:10.960
Oh? No reaction?

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:13.160
But you're obviously a Kansai girl!

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:12.350
You look nothing alike!

00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:13.710
You're a totally different genre!

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:19.070
Assuming the family register is true, 
she is my honest-to-goodness

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:22.820
little sister, y'all. I think.

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:23.930
Whaddaya mean "Ya think"?!

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:25.890
Well, anyhow, about those buzzers...

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:29.820
Now I've seen the real deal,

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:31.940
I bet I can make a set.

00:19:32.310 --> 00:19:33.160
Make a set?

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:34.400
Of buzzers?

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:36.380
Th-That's impossible!

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:35.690
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:35.690
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:36.440
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:36.440
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:38.490
Making a button would be one thing,

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:37.190
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:37.190
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:37.940
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:37.940
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:38.690
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:38.690
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:41.180
but the control box infrastructure 
is really complicated!

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:39.440
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:39.440
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:40.200
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:40.200
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:40.950
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:40.950
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:41.700
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:41.700
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:43.980
It's not enough for it to just light 
up when you push the button!

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:42.450
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:42.450
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:43.200
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:43.200
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:43.950
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:43.950
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:44.700
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:44.700
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:46.100
Do you even know how 
interlocking circuits work?

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:45.450
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:45.450
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:46.200
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:46.200
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:48.050
Do you understand sequencing?!

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:46.950
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:46.950
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:47.700
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:47.700
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:48.450
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:48.450
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:48.050 --> 00:19:49.870
A beginner doesn't have a hope in hell!

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:49.200
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:49.200
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:49.870
<b>Nag</b>

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:49.870
<b>Gripe</b>

00:19:49.870 --> 00:19:52.080
O-Oh, right...

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:55.670
You forgot this in the library at lunch.

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:57.380
Oh! I knew it!

00:19:56.170 --> 00:19:57.380
Sequential Control
Circuits 
Practical Applications

00:19:57.380 --> 00:20:00.840
Thank goodness! I only just bought it!

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:05.270
I saw instructions on how to make 
a buzzer system in that book.

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:07.720
It looks really difficult, don't you think?

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:11.140
C-Can you really do it, Sasajima-san?

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:13.600
Call me Jinko.

00:20:14.050 --> 00:20:17.270
Well, rather than makin' it
 with a sequencer,

00:20:17.270 --> 00:20:20.810
it's better to use one-chip microcomputers,
 like they use in the control boxes.

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:23.270
S'easier to do, and cheaper, too.

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:26.650
Is it okay if it's just one-way? If so...

00:20:26.650 --> 00:20:27.990
Yeah, go with that.

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:33.290
Why do y'all look so bewildered?

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:39.450
Just so ya know, you can trust Sasajima Jinko,
 the human low-current electronic device,

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:41.630
with all your homemade buzzer needs!

00:20:41.890 --> 00:20:43.340
"L-Low-current"?

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:46.410
Y-You're the type of girl 
that's into electrical craft?!

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:50.560
One of the gap moe "low-current girls" 
you very rarely see in Akiba?!

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:52.060
A-A "low-current" otaku...

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:55.060
<i>I guess fanaticism comes in many forms...</i>

00:22:30.610 --> 00:22:32.920
One thing you can't have quiz bowl without

00:22:32.920 --> 00:22:33.990
is a set of buzzers.

00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:37.500
It's something all high 
school quiz teams want.

00:22:37.850 --> 00:22:40.250
I'll make one for y'all! Leave it to me!

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:42.750
There it is! The low-current girl!

00:22:41.500 --> 00:22:42.750
<b>Low-Current Girl</b>

00:22:43.170 --> 00:22:47.510
Since I'm doin' this, it'd be cool if ya could 
tell at a glance who each buzzer is for.

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:50.010
Maybe by drawing things they like on them?

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:53.690
Good idea! And Gakuto 
likes quiz bowl, right?

00:22:53.690 --> 00:22:57.020
And Marippe likes quiz bowl, 
and Shiki-kun likes quiz bowl, too.

00:22:57.360 --> 00:22:59.410
Sounds like they'll all end 
up looking the same...
